course: dictionary
lesson: 11277
title: Wrist
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Your wrist is the joint between your hand and your arm. It lets you bend your hand up and down and turn it round. «Falling onto one straight, stiff arm often breaks the wrist.» Here the wrist is the part of your body that gets hurt, the joint near your hand.
explain: Use the clues around a word. In that sentence you fall, your arm is straight and stiff, and something breaks near your hand. Try your meaning in the sentence: "Falling onto one straight, stiff arm often breaks the joint between your hand and arm." It still makes sense, so you have found the right meaning.
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. A wrist is also the part of a sleeve that covers this joint. Phrases can change the meaning too. A «slap on the wrist» is not about hands at all: it means a small punishment.
fact: The wrist is a joint made of many small bones.
fact: The wrist lets your hand bend and turn.
fact: You can feel your pulse on the inside of your wrist.
fact: Wrist guards protect your wrists when you go skating.
fact: «Wrist» can also mean the part of a sleeve that covers the joint.
exercise: «Falling onto one straight, stiff arm often breaks the wrist.» Where is the wrist? | The top of your head | *The joint between your hand and your arm | The bone in your leg | The inside of your elbow
exercise: «Each squeeze sends a wave along your arteries, and that wave is the pulse you can feel in your wrist after a race.» Where can you feel your pulse here? | On top of your knee | In your big toe | *On the inside of your wrist | In your ear
exercise: Why do skaters wear wrist guards? | To keep their sleeves clean | To help them bend their knees | *To protect their wrists if they fall | To warm their hands
test: What does «a slap on the wrist» mean? | *A small punishment | A hard hit on the hand | A type of watch | A kind pat on the back
test: Which activity is a good reason to wear wrist guards? | *Skating | Reading | Eating soup | Sleeping
exam: Why is putting a straight, stiff arm out to stop a fall risky? | It keeps your sleeves dry | *It can break your wrist | It makes the fall longer | It makes you laugh
